1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:02,520
Bill Gates changed his mind about

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,720
artificial intelligence and jobs.

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,220
3 years ago, he called

4
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:08,510
the risks real, but manageable.

5
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:10,470
Now he warns of far fewer

6
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:12,040
jobs than exist today.

7
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:13,490
No job, he says,

8
00:00:13,530 --> 00:00:15,050
will be left untouched.

9
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:16,360
Jobs at a screen.

10
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,690
Jobs done by hand.

11
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,050
Whatever your job is,

12
00:00:19,090 --> 00:00:20,340
he means yours too.

13
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,620
So what did Bill Gates see?

14
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,320
His own words: "This is crazy.

15
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:26,550
This is insane."

16
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:28,120
He's shocked, he says,

17
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:30,080
to be one of the first saying it.

18
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:32,280
And deafened by the silence.

19
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:34,020
But he doesn't want

20
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:35,670
to stop the machines.

21
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:37,880
He wants certain work set aside.

22
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:39,040
For people only.

23
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:41,080
Even where a machine could do it.

24
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:42,730
Think of it as a list.

25
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:45,000
He calls it Human Reserved.

26
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:46,480
Your job could be on it.

27
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:47,690
Or left off.

28
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:49,510
The list hasn't been written yet.

29
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:51,370
This month,

30
00:00:51,410 --> 00:00:53,140
at a Financial Times event,

31
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:54,540
he put it plainly.

32
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:56,520
I say very strongly that

33
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:00,580
past technological advances

34
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:02,520
are no guide to this.

35
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:04,120
That will just mislead you

36
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:05,940
if you look at those.

37
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:07,980
What changed is his

38
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,460
picture of the machine.

39
00:01:09,670 --> 00:01:11,480
Just weeks ago, he said why this

40
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:12,740
technology is different

41
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:13,900
from past ones.

42
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:16,220
It exceeds human intelligence.

43
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,470
Earlier machines took over tasks,

44
00:01:18,510 --> 00:01:19,820
and people moved on to work

45
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:21,260
the machines couldn't do.

46
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:22,360
That's why he called

47
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:23,740
the risks manageable.

48
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:26,040
This time, wherever people move,

49
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:27,510
the machine can follow.

50
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:29,330
That's what Bill Gates saw.

51
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:31,250
He says he'd love to be convinced

52
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:32,990
he's wrong about the job market.

53
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:34,530
But he's not convinced.

54
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:36,440
He has put his reputation on it.

55
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:38,780
So where does it begin?

56
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:41,260
His warning comes with a timeline.

57
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:42,510
3 stops.

58
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:43,810
The last is the one

59
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:45,250
that worries him most.

60
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:47,210
The first is now.

61
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:49,980
For Gates, the first work

62
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:51,930
to go is work at a screen.

63
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:53,050
He names it.

64
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:54,050
Phone sales.

65
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:55,160
Phone support.

66
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,200
Accounting.

67
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,430
Artificial intelligence, he says,

68
00:01:58,470 --> 00:01:59,520
does that work cheaper,

69
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,530
faster and more accurately.

70
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:02,650
For one group,

71
00:02:02,690 --> 00:02:04,870
this stop is already in the data.

72
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:06,150
Young people, looking

73
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:07,450
for their first job.

74
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:08,650
In the jobs most

75
00:02:08,690 --> 00:02:09,760
exposed to artificial

76
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,600
intelligence, their employment has

77
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:13,500
fallen about 19 percent

78
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,170
behind people

79
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:16,560
the same age in less exposed jobs.

80
00:02:16,770 --> 00:02:18,690
And the gap is still growing.

81
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:20,600
If that's you,

82
00:02:20,670 --> 00:02:22,550
this stop isn't ahead of you.

83
00:02:22,630 --> 00:02:23,980
You're standing in it.

84
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:25,120
But the same data

85
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,530
shows something else.

86
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:27,480
Where the technology

87
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:29,080
works alongside people,

88
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:30,580
instead of replacing them,

89
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:33,170
employment holds steady, or grows.

90
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:33,900
So the machine

91
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,620
alone doesn't decide.

92
00:02:35,710 --> 00:02:37,400
How it's used does.

93
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:39,630
So the first stop is the screen.

94
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:40,850
Which sounds like good news

95
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:41,800
for anyone who works

96
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:42,900
with their hands.

97
00:02:44,350 --> 00:02:46,230
That's the usual comfort.

98
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:48,390
A machine can answer a phone.

99
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:49,670
It can't lay bricks,

100
00:02:49,710 --> 00:02:51,270
or bring in a harvest.

101
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:52,930
Gates gives that comfort

102
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:54,390
2 to 8 years.

103
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:55,920
That's the second stop:

104
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:57,810
his window for humanoid robots

105
00:02:57,850 --> 00:02:59,830
on building sites and farms.

106
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:01,730
This fall, on a podcast,

107
00:03:01,770 --> 00:03:03,370
he said where that leads.

108
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:05,980
If we don't choose

109
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:09,580
to massively slow down or stop,

110
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:12,740
then we will have robots

111
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:13,880
that can build houses,

112
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:16,820
grow food, do surgery,

113
00:03:17,390 --> 00:03:18,390
Houses.

114
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:19,460
Food.

115
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:20,990
Work done with hands.

116
00:03:21,330 --> 00:03:22,370
But go back to how

117
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:23,990
his sentence begins.

118
00:03:24,230 --> 00:03:25,910
If we don't choose.

119
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:28,820
He isn't describing the weather.

120
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:30,870
He's describing a decision.

121
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:33,000
Machines like these are built,

122
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:35,300
bought and switched on by people.

123
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:37,560
Gates doesn't expect a halt.

124
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:39,300
But how fast they come,

125
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:41,170
and where, is a choice.

126
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:42,510
Before the last stop

127
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:43,380
on his timeline,

128
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:45,210
though, a fair question.

129
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:46,880
Is he even right?

130
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:49,940
Start with who's talking.

131
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:52,490
Bill Gates co-founded Microsoft,

132
00:03:52,530 --> 00:03:54,010
one of the biggest shareholders

133
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:56,200
in the company behind ChatGPT.

134
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:57,580
So he is warning

135
00:03:57,620 --> 00:03:58,490
about a technology

136
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:00,640
his old company profits from.

137
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:01,520
That makes the warning

138
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,020
harder to wave away.

139
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:04,890
Though a skeptic would add:

140
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:06,400
strict rules often suit

141
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:07,780
the biggest players.

142
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:09,830
Then the numbers.

143
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:12,290
That 19 percent gap is about

144
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:13,660
the youngest workers,

145
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:15,610
in the most exposed jobs.

146
00:04:15,910 --> 00:04:16,710
Across the whole

147
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:18,170
American job market,

148
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:19,350
researchers at Yale

149
00:04:19,390 --> 00:04:20,630
see something else.

150
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:23,040
As of this spring, their verdict:

151
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:25,170
where the job market is weakening,

152
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,370
artificial intelligence

153
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:27,150
is probably

154
00:04:27,190 --> 00:04:28,390
not the reason.

155
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:29,540
Not yet.

156
00:04:29,710 --> 00:04:31,570
And Gates doesn't claim otherwise.

157
00:04:31,770 --> 00:04:32,830
By his own account,

158
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:34,800
his warning is a forecast.

159
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:36,910
Not a finding in today's numbers.

160
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:38,730
So here is what

161
00:04:38,770 --> 00:04:40,000
the evidence supports.

162
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:42,370
The direction: plausible.

163
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:43,100
At the start

164
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:45,540
of careers: already measurable.

165
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:48,210
The speed and the size: open.

166
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:49,410
A forecast from

167
00:04:49,450 --> 00:04:51,000
a well-informed man.

168
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:51,980
One who changed

169
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:53,390
his mind in public.

170
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:55,210
Not a date in your calendar.

171
00:04:56,370 --> 00:04:57,400
This channel exists

172
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:58,740
for one reason:

173
00:04:58,900 --> 00:04:59,780
so that the arrival

174
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:00,940
of superintelligence

175
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:02,780
goes well for everybody.

176
00:05:02,910 --> 00:05:03,660
Wherever your job

177
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:05,080
sits on that timeline.

178
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:06,360
Want that too?

179
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:07,510
Then subscribe.

180
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:09,850
That leaves the last

181
00:05:09,890 --> 00:05:11,290
stop on his timeline.

182
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,490
The one that worries him most.

183
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:14,950
It isn't a date.

184
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:16,490
It's a stretch of time.

185
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:18,070
On that same podcast,

186
00:05:18,110 --> 00:05:19,290
he said how long.

187
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,440
My big concern is this

188
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,040
20-year transition period,

189
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,240
where it'll have

190
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:26,360
winners and losers

191
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,290
and a lot of disruption,

192
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:31,560
20 years of winners and losers.

193
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:32,620
If you're in the middle

194
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:33,960
of your working life,

195
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,630
that's not a transition.

196
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:37,020
That's the rest of it.

197
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:38,540
That stretch is what

198
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:39,780
his plan is for.

199
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:40,730
Remember the warning

200
00:05:40,770 --> 00:05:41,770
from the start?

201
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:43,680
Far fewer jobs.

202
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:45,680
His essay adds a condition:

203
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:47,680
without the right policies.

204
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:49,390
The list is one of them.

205
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:51,180
Another is about money.

206
00:05:51,350 --> 00:05:52,750
Today, he argues,

207
00:05:52,790 --> 00:05:53,730
the tax system

208
00:05:53,770 --> 00:05:55,180
pushes the wrong way.

209
00:05:55,410 --> 00:05:56,420
Hire a person,

210
00:05:56,460 --> 00:05:57,460
and the company pays

211
00:05:57,500 --> 00:05:58,890
taxes on the wage.

212
00:05:59,030 --> 00:06:00,430
Buy a robot instead,

213
00:06:00,470 --> 00:06:01,350
and it can usually be

214
00:06:01,390 --> 00:06:02,980
written off right away.

215
00:06:03,570 --> 00:06:06,380
His fix: tax the machine's work.

216
00:06:06,710 --> 00:06:08,210
Tax artificial intelligence

217
00:06:08,250 --> 00:06:09,380
and robots by how

218
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:10,620
much they're used.

219
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:12,450
The money goes into retraining

220
00:06:12,490 --> 00:06:14,200
and a stronger safety net.

221
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:15,790
The artificial intelligence

222
00:06:15,830 --> 00:06:17,620
researcher Oren Etzioni

223
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:18,710
says Gates has

224
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:20,220
the diagnosis right,

225
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:21,900
but the prescription wrong,

226
00:06:22,110 --> 00:06:23,590
at least in some places.

227
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:24,930
Taxing the machine's

228
00:06:24,970 --> 00:06:26,170
work, he says,

229
00:06:26,210 --> 00:06:27,730
is like taxing keystrokes

230
00:06:27,770 --> 00:06:28,850
on a typewriter.

231
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:30,240
It counts how hard

232
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,370
the machine works.

233
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,450
Not whether anyone lost a job.

234
00:06:34,070 --> 00:06:35,440
And even a perfect

235
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:36,900
tax leaves a gap.

236
00:06:37,250 --> 00:06:39,120
Money can replace a paycheck.

237
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:41,240
It can't replace being needed.

238
00:06:41,750 --> 00:06:42,230
That's where his

239
00:06:42,270 --> 00:06:43,810
second idea comes in.

240
00:06:44,060 --> 00:06:45,060
The list.

241
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:47,320
Human Reserved.

242
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:49,670
In his words: as artificial

243
00:06:49,710 --> 00:06:52,120
intelligence and robots improve,

244
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:53,310
we'll set aside certain

245
00:06:53,350 --> 00:06:55,500
things for only people to do.

246
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:57,730
The candidates named so far:

247
00:06:58,080 --> 00:06:59,520
looking after children.

248
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:01,080
Sitting on a jury.

249
00:07:01,370 --> 00:07:02,490
Making art.

250
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:04,860
And in teaching, a mix:

251
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,590
a person stays in charge,

252
00:07:06,630 --> 00:07:08,680
and uses the machine as a tool.

253
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:10,020
Take the jury.

254
00:07:10,230 --> 00:07:12,260
Imagine the verdict is about you.

255
00:07:12,550 --> 00:07:13,510
A machine might weigh

256
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:14,960
the evidence faster.

257
00:07:15,230 --> 00:07:16,080
Would you still want

258
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:17,320
people to decide?

259
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:18,940
Human Reserved would turn

260
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:21,190
an answer like that into a rule.

261
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:23,860
How long could such a list get?

262
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:24,980
Take a guess.

263
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:26,680
His own ceiling:

264
00:07:26,870 --> 00:07:28,740
in a very extreme form,

265
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:30,270
40 percent of jobs could be

266
00:07:30,310 --> 00:07:32,530
reserved for humans, at first.

267
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:33,830
And that, he says,

268
00:07:33,870 --> 00:07:35,660
is as high as he can get.

269
00:07:35,750 --> 00:07:37,860
So even at its most generous,

270
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:39,940
the list leaves most jobs off.

271
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,080
Maybe yours.

272
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:42,520
Which ones make it?

273
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:43,720
In his essay,

274
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:45,460
Gates asks that himself.

275
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:47,140
Who gets to decide what

276
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:48,780
we reserve for humans?

277
00:07:48,870 --> 00:07:50,770
What criteria should we use?

278
00:07:51,330 --> 00:07:52,630
By his own account,

279
00:07:52,670 --> 00:07:54,650
those questions are still open.

280
00:07:54,850 --> 00:07:56,060
And so is a third:

281
00:07:56,370 --> 00:07:57,460
how to keep companies

282
00:07:57,500 --> 00:07:59,500
from replacing people anyway.

283
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:01,690
Critics add: a line like

284
00:08:01,730 --> 00:08:03,270
this is hard to draw,

285
00:08:03,310 --> 00:08:04,900
and open to lobbying.

286
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:06,530
And reserved doesn't

287
00:08:06,570 --> 00:08:07,870
mean well paid.

288
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:08,880
Care work and

289
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:10,470
teaching often aren't.

290
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,850
So the list is unwritten.

291
00:08:13,090 --> 00:08:14,290
Hard to draw.

292
00:08:14,530 --> 00:08:16,520
And too short for most jobs.

293
00:08:16,790 --> 00:08:18,140
Why does it matter, then?

294
00:08:18,750 --> 00:08:21,220
Ask who decides, right now,

295
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:23,020
which work stays human.

296
00:08:23,330 --> 00:08:25,100
Mostly, nobody does.

297
00:08:25,230 --> 00:08:26,320
The price does.

298
00:08:26,520 --> 00:08:27,440
If a machine can do

299
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:28,660
the work for less,

300
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:29,820
the machine gets it.

301
00:08:30,070 --> 00:08:31,450
A human job is whatever

302
00:08:31,490 --> 00:08:32,490
is left over.

303
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:34,680
Chances are, that includes yours.

304
00:08:34,930 --> 00:08:36,090
No rule ever said

305
00:08:36,130 --> 00:08:37,590
a person has to do it.

306
00:08:37,710 --> 00:08:38,670
A person has simply

307
00:08:38,710 --> 00:08:39,780
been the best deal.

308
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:40,940
So far.

309
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:43,020
Human Reserved asks something

310
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:44,410
no price can answer.

311
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:46,400
Not: what does it cost?

312
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:48,690
But: why should a person do this?

313
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:50,830
Try a line yourself.

314
00:08:50,950 --> 00:08:52,520
Which work would you put on it?

315
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:55,600
And, harder: what's your reason?

316
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:57,430
That reason is exactly what

317
00:08:57,470 --> 00:08:59,050
the list is still missing.

318
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:02,390
Now look past those 20 years.

319
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:03,680
At the far end,

320
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:05,630
Gates sees abundance.

321
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:07,810
Cheaper homes, cheaper food,

322
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:08,660
because most goods

323
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:10,010
and services would no longer

324
00:09:10,050 --> 00:09:11,550
depend on human labor.

325
00:09:11,790 --> 00:09:13,890
And one task stays with people.

326
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:15,570
Humanity, he says,

327
00:09:15,670 --> 00:09:16,290
will have to find

328
00:09:16,330 --> 00:09:17,680
a way to organize,

329
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:19,090
and to have purpose.

330
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:20,780
A list like Human Reserved

331
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:22,190
is one way to begin.

332
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:23,870
But a list nobody writes

333
00:09:23,910 --> 00:09:25,290
still gets filled in.

334
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:26,490
By price.

335
00:09:26,530 --> 00:09:28,580
One cheaper machine at a time.

336
00:09:29,190 --> 00:09:31,250
And so far, Gates says,

337
00:09:31,310 --> 00:09:33,480
what he hears is silence.

338
00:09:33,670 --> 00:09:34,740
Which means every

339
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:36,430
line is still open.

340
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:39,170
The question is no longer what

341
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:41,340
artificial intelligence can do.

342
00:09:41,650 --> 00:09:43,310
It's what goes on the list.

343
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:45,140
And who gets to write it.

344
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:47,390
This channel exists

345
00:09:47,430 --> 00:09:48,750
for one reason:

346
00:09:48,860 --> 00:09:49,710
so that the arrival

347
00:09:49,750 --> 00:09:50,850
of superintelligence

348
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:52,690
goes well for everybody.

349
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:54,450
And so that everybody gets

350
00:09:54,490 --> 00:09:56,490
a say in what stays human.

351
00:09:56,660 --> 00:09:57,660
Want that too?

352
00:09:57,750 --> 00:09:58,750
Then subscribe.

